-- REFUSE examples.
--
-- A refusal is the model declining to answer, with a reason. It is not a
-- value, not an evaluation error, and not an unknown fact the caller can
-- supply -- so nothing downstream can convert it into an answer, and it
-- appears in no export schema.

IMPORT prelude

-- House style: one NAMED definition per refusal, named for what is not
-- covered, so the arms that reach it read as the source text does.

GIVETH A NUMBER
`this schedule is not encoded for years before 2000` MEANS
    REFUSE "this schedule is not encoded for years before 2000"

GIVEN y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the fee in year` y MEANS
    IF y AT LEAST 2000
    THEN 100
    ELSE `this schedule is not encoded for years before 2000`

#ASSERT `the fee in year` 2001 EQUALS 100
#ASSERT REFUSED `the fee in year` 1999
#ASSERT REFUSED `the fee in year` 1999 BECAUSE "this schedule is not encoded for years before 2000"

-- A refusal used at more than one type is declared polymorphic. Without
-- `GIVEN a IS A TYPE` a 0-ary definition takes the first type it is used at.

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`this rule has not been written yet` MEANS
    REFUSE "this rule has not been written yet"

GIVETH A NUMBER
`the amount` MEANS 1 PLUS `this rule has not been written yet`

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is eligible` MEANS TRUE AND `this rule has not been written yet`

#ASSERT REFUSED `the amount`
#ASSERT REFUSED `is eligible`

-- Uncatchable: no rule observes a refusal. A CONSIDER must force its
-- scrutinee, so neither arm is reached.

GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the optional amount is not modelled` MEANS
    REFUSE "the optional amount is not modelled"

GIVETH A NUMBER
`consider over a refusing maybe` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the optional amount is not modelled`
      WHEN NOTHING THEN 0
      WHEN JUST n  THEN n

#ASSERT REFUSED `consider over a refusing maybe`

-- ...but DEMAND ORDER decides whether the refusal is reached at all. AND and
-- OR are lazy and left to right.

#ASSERT NOT (FALSE AND `is eligible`)
#ASSERT REFUSED (`is eligible` AND FALSE)
